1. `libc::printf` with `name`: `name` is a C string (`Ptr`, `Imm`, `Transient`). It becomes `&str`, and `print!` or `println!` replaces `printf`.
2. `*matrix.offset(i)`: `matrix` is `Arr` of `Arr` of `Imm` pointers, so it is a 2D array. It becomes `&[Vec<i32>]`, or `&[&[i32]]`. I chose `&[Vec<i32>]` because it is the most common owned layout, though `&[&[i32]]` would work too. Row access is then safe indexing.
3. `*row.offset(j)`: this becomes safe slice indexing, so no raw pointers are left.
4. `size` is the row count and also the column count. The idiomatic version derives the row count from `matrix.len()`. To preserve the original behavior (a square `size x size` matrix), each row is iterated with `take(size)` where `size = matrix.len()`.
5. `%5d `: this becomes `{:5} `, which right-aligns to width 5.

No `unsafe` is needed.

----FUNCTION----
```rust
pub fn print_matrix(matrix: &[Vec<i32>], name: &str) {
    let size = matrix.len();
    println!("Matrix {}:", name);
    for row in matrix.iter().take(size) {
        for value in row.iter().take(size) {
            print!("{:5} ", value);
        }
        println!();
    }
    println!();
}
```
